\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{A sufficient positive-half decomposition}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let a finite set #{X} have an involution #{\theta} and halves
#{X_-=\theta X_+} with #{X_+\cup X_-=X} and
#{X_+\cap X_-=\operatorname{Fix}(\theta)=X_0}.
Use the observable conventions of \ref{fdip-007M}; both torus
reflections there have these properties.
Let #{\nu} be a probability measure supported on
a compact interval of #{\mathbb R}, and put
#{\mu_0=\bigotimes_{v\in X}\nu}. Suppose a real Hamiltonian
has the form
##{-H=A+\Theta A+\sum_{\alpha=1}^q C_\alpha\Theta C_\alpha,}
where #{A,C_1,\ldots,C_q} are bounded real functions in
#{\mathcal A_+}; the sum may be empty. Then the probability law
##{d\mu=Z^{-1}e^{-H}\,d\mu_0,\qquad
 Z=\int e^{-H}\,d\mu_0}
is reflection invariant and reflection positive. This is the
complex-observable version of
\citet{Lemma 10.8, pp. 442–443}{friedli2017statistical}.}

\proof{
\p{First consider the product reference. Conditional on the field
#{b=\phi|_{X_0}} at the fixed sites, the two interiors are independent
and have matching product laws under reflection. The boundary field
is a single shared variable. For any bounded positive-half
observable #{F}, therefore,
##{\int(\Theta F)F\,d\mu_0
 =\int\left|\int F(\phi_+,b)\,d\nu_+(\phi_+)\right|^2
 \,d\nu_0(b)\ge0.}
Here #{\nu_+} is the product over #{X_+\setminus X_0} and
#{\nu_0} the product over #{X_0}. When #{X_0} is empty the
outer integral is evaluation at the unique empty configuration.
This also proves the product case of
\citet{Lemma 10.7, p. 442}{friedli2017statistical}.}

\p{The displayed decomposition is invariant under reflection
because its functions are real and multiplication commutes.
The reference law is invariant as well. Boundedness makes
#{0<Z<\infty} and permits uniform absolute expansion of the
crossing exponential. For #{f\in\mathcal A_+}, this gives
##{\begin{aligned}
ZB_\mu(f,f)
 &=\sum_{n\in\mathbb N^q}\frac1{n!}
   B_{\mu_0}\left(fe^A C^n,fe^A C^n\right),\\
n!&=\prod_\alpha n_\alpha!,\qquad
C^n=\prod_\alpha C_\alpha^{n_\alpha}.
\end{aligned}}
Every summand is nonnegative by the conditional product identity.
The sum converges absolutely by the bounded exponential majorant,
so the claimed positivity follows.}
}
